.TH PAM-DEVICE-AUTH 8 "2026-07-12" "0.5.6" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam-device-auth \- two-factor SSH login (directory key + OIDC device grant)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pam-device-auth
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
[\fB\-\-setup\-ldap\fR]
[\fB\-\-enable\fR]
[\fB\-\-debug\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam-device-auth
provides two-factor SSH login: an SSH key served from an LDAP directory
.RB ( publickey )
plus the OIDC Device Authorization Grant (RFC 8628). Identity, groups, home
directory and the sudo password come from the directory via SSSD/NSS.
pam-device-auth is the OIDC layer: it validates an access token (signature,
issuer, client binding, expiry, required role, optional IP allowlist) for the
SSH user and grants or denies the login. It never reads
.IR /etc/shadow ,
prompts for a local password, or creates users.
.PP
For the OIDC factor the user sees a URL and code in their terminal, authorizes
in a browser, and the session is granted. The Go binary is invoked by the C PAM
module
.RB ( pam_device_auth.so )
during SSH keyboard-interactive authentication, and by the account phase for
the IP allowlist check (see below). It is not typically run directly by
users, except for the
.BR \-\-check ,
.B \-\-setup\-ldap
and
.B \-\-enable
commands. The
.B \-\-pam\-acct
mode is invoked internally by the PAM stack and is not meant for interactive use.
.PP
Two directory group tiers control SSH access: members of
.I access_group
log in with both factors (key + OIDC); members of
.I service_account_groups
are service/automation accounts that log in key-only, with no OIDC step.
Sudo is a separate axis:
.I admin_group
gets password sudo via pam_sss, and
.I nopasswd_sudo_groups
gets passwordless sudo. A user needs membership in at least one of the
access-tier groups to log in at all.
.PP
Every login, including key-only service accounts, is checked against a
per-user source-IP/CIDR allowlist read from the directory
.I clients
attribute, enforced in the PAM account phase over the SSSD InfoPipe
(busctl). Root and other local users are exempt from this check
(break-glass); it never touches SSSD for them. The check fails closed: if a
directory user's
.I clients
lookup fails, that login is denied, so an InfoPipe/SSSD outage denies all
directory users, not just the ones with an allowlist set.
.PP
Works on OpenSSH 10.2+, which refuses PAM for users not resolvable via
.BR getpwnam (3).
Directory users resolve through NSS, so they always exist.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Run the pre-activation preflight: validate the config, test OIDC discovery and
issuer match, verify the directory path (SSSD running, access group resolves via
NSS, every access-group member has a valid
.IR sshPublicKey ),
the sshd factors (AuthorizedKeysCommand, non-root 2FA, root break-glass), IP
allowlist posture, and SSSD InfoPipe health.
Each item is reported as [OK]/[WARN]/[FAIL]; exits non-zero if any blocker is found.
.TP
.B \-\-setup\-ldap
Configure directory identity from the
.I ldap
block of the config: write
.IR /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
(including the InfoPipe
.I ifp
responder needed for the IP allowlist check), add
.B sss
to nsswitch, enable
.BR pam_mkhomedir ,
install the sudoers rules for the admin and NOPASSWD sudo groups, write the
sshd drop-in, and migrate any pre-existing local accounts that shadow a
directory user. Distro-aware (apt/dnf, pam-auth-update/authselect, CA path,
.BR sss_ssh_authorizedkeys
path); on Debian/Ubuntu it also installs the
.B sssd-dbus
package needed for the InfoPipe responder. Does not restart sshd. Idempotent.
Requires root.
.TP
.B \-\-enable
Activate PAM authentication. Backs up
.IR /etc/pam.d/sshd ,
installs the PAM config, copies the sshd PAM template, and restarts sshd.
Requires root.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Enable verbose logging to
.IR /var/log/pam-device-auth.log .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.PP
An additional
.B \-\-pam\-acct
mode exists for the PAM account phase to enforce the IP allowlist; it is
invoked internally by the PAM stack and not meant to be run by hand.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration file:
.IR /etc/pam-device-auth/config.json .
.PP
.TS
l l l l.
Field	Required	Default	Description
_
issuer_url	Yes	\-	OIDC issuer URL
client_id	Yes	\-	OAuth2 client ID (public)
required_role	Yes	\-	Role claim value for SSH access
sudo_role	No	\-	Directory group/role for sudo
role_claim	No	\-	Extra JWT claim path for roles
auth_timeout	No	180	Device flow timeout (30\-240s)
allowed_algorithms	No	\-	Pin accepted JWT alg (e.g. ["RS256"])
show_qr	No	auto	QR display: true/false/auto-detect
ip_claim	No	\-	JWT claim with allowed IPs/CIDRs
ldap	setup	\-	Directory block for \-\-setup\-ldap (see below)
.TE
.PP
The
.I ldap
block (used only by
.BR \-\-setup\-ldap )
holds
.IR uri ", " base_dn ", " bind_dn ", " bind_password ", " access_group
and
.IR admin_group ,
plus two opt-in lists:
.I service_account_groups
(directory groups whose members log in key-only, with no OIDC step) and
.I nopasswd_sudo_groups
(groups granted passwordless sudo). The read-only bind password is written
into the root-only
.I /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
for SSSD's runtime use and is not read by the auth runtime.
.PP
Per-user source-IP allowlisting uses the directory
.I clients
attribute (set on the LLDAP user entry, not in config.json) and is enforced
for every login by the PAM account phase.
.PP
Every field can be overridden via environment variables prefixed with
.BR PAM_DEVICE_AUTH_ .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pam-device-auth/config.json
OIDC provider and directory configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/security/pam_device_auth.so
C PAM shared library (deb; rpm installs to
.IR /usr/lib64/security ).
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/pam-device-auth
Go binary.
.TP
.I /run/pam-device-auth/
Token cache directory (tmpfs, cleared on reboot). Mode 0700 root:root.
.TP
.I /var/log/pam-device-auth.log
Authentication log file.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/10-pam-device-auth.conf
sshd configuration (LogLevel DEBUG1, KbdInteractiveAuthentication yes).
.TP
.I /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
Directory wiring written by \-\-setup\-ldap (0600 root), including the
InfoPipe responder used for the IP allowlist check. SSSD reads it at runtime.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/pam-device-auth
sudo rules for the admin and NOPASSWD sudo groups, written by
\-\-setup\-ldap and removed on uninstall.
.SH AUTHENTICATION FLOW
.PP
Non-root logins in
.I access_group
require both factors:
.B publickey
(the user's key, served from the directory by
.BR sss_ssh_authorizedkeys )
.B and
.B keyboard-interactive
(the OIDC device grant). Members of
.I service_account_groups
log in with the key alone; every login, key-only or not, is still subject
to the
.I clients
IP allowlist check in the PAM account phase.
.PP
.B First login / new IP:
full OIDC Device Authorization Grant. User sees URL + code (+ QR code if the
client supports UTF-8), authorizes in a browser. The token is re-validated
(required role, IP allowlist) and the login is granted.
.PP
.B Known IP:
silent OIDC token refresh, re-validated, no browser round-trip. The SSH key
factor is always required.
.PP
.B Root and other local users:
SSH key only
.RB ( "AuthenticationMethods publickey" )
against the local authorized_keys, exempt from the
.I clients
IP allowlist check. This is the break-glass path, independent of
OIDC/SSSD/LDAP availability.
.SH SECURITY
.PP
Two factors (directory SSH key + OIDC), with full token validation on every
login: signature, issuer, azp/aud, exp/nbf/iat, required role and IP allowlist;
JWT algorithm/key-type binding, kid required, no none/HMAC. The per-user
.I clients
IP allowlist is enforced separately in the PAM account phase and fails
closed on a directory/InfoPipe lookup error. Directory
revocation: removing a user from the access group denies new logins and sudo
within the SSSD cache TTL. sudo re-authenticates against the directory password
via pam_sss, except for members of
.IR nopasswd_sudo_groups .
The C PAM shim uses fork/exec (no shell), clearenv with an env
whitelist, pipe2(O_CLOEXEC), a per-call timeout, SIGPIPE handling and log
sanitization. The refresh-token cache lives in tmpfs (0700 root:root, cleared
on reboot) with the KnownIPs list capped (FIFO).
.PP
See
.I SECURITY.md
in the source repository for the full threat model.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.PP
Ubuntu 24.04+, Debian 13+, or Rocky Linux 9 or 10 (amd64); other RHEL-family
distributions are generally compatible. OpenSSH >= 9.6p1.
.B LogLevel DEBUG1
in sshd_config is required for SSH client auto-detection (QR code display).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam (8),
.BR sshd (8),
.BR sshd_config (5),
.BR pam.conf (5)
